How to create state in class component?

Class has a constructor function.Whenever you create a class,create a constructor and call super function in it
"Constructor is a place that is used to do initialization"
class ProfileComponent extends React.Component {
constructor(props){           //hamesa aisa he likha jai ga
super(props);                 //hamesa aisa he likha jai ga
}
  render() {
    return (
      <div>
        ProfileComponent <h1>{this.props.userName}</h1>{" "}
      </div>
    );
  }
}

Whenever a new instance of class is created or Whenever a class is invoked or whenever  a component is rendered,constructor is automatically called.This is the best place to create your state.React give us access to create state by this.state.See below example

import React from "react";

class ProfileComponent extends React.Component {
    constructor(props){
        super(props);
        this.state={
            count:0
        }
    }
  render() {
    return (
      <div>
        ProfileComponent <h1>{this.props.userName}</h1>{" "}
        <h2>{this.state.count}</h2>
        <button>Change Count 
      </div>
    );
  }
}
export default ProfileComponent;

=>We can update our state by setState method (see below example)

import React from "react";

class ProfileComponent extends React.Component {
    constructor(props){
        super(props);
        this.state={
            count:0
        }
    }
  render() {
    return (
      <div>
        ProfileComponent <h1>{this.props.userName}</h1>{" "}
        <h2>{this.state.count}</h2>
        <button onClick={()=>{
        //  this.state.count=1; we cannot mutate/change state directly because in this case ,react did not take a track of it, we have to use react given methods
         this.setState({
            count:1
         })
        }}>Change State</button>
      </div>
    );
  }
}
export default ProfileComponent;

